{
  "dom_id": "step-q108293",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/b00882caec033394ff22a01df1f8cc417a8336b475962e73662316c3091229fa.png)\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the function given by the graph above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=4$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/ed1ad21056cf9c05c621180126387f2e07a1cd349f151726142fb33d2b514e44.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(-2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the value of the function at $x=1$ is the same as the values of the function at the other points of the interval, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/ff8960289497889b1fb046c05b0bb2243a8776c435bb685e3c94fb7fe832e056.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local maximum at $x=4.$ For example, in the interval $(3.75,4.25),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/b2d1f1b6b065c5d3511ec02350eae1a867b9473fa2ac7c1a1cb9458d1421fe03.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(4)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (3.75,4.25).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
